{{Short description|Astronomy of the Muisca civilization}}
{{Muisca sidebar}}
This article describes the '''astronomy''' of the '''Muisca'''. The [[Muisca people|Muisca]], one of the four advanced civilisations in the [[Americas]]<ref>Ocampo López, 2007, Ch.V, p.226</ref> before the [[Spanish conquest of the Muisca]], had a thorough understanding of [[archaeoastronomy|astronomy]], as evidenced by their [[Muisca architecture|architecture]] and [[Muisca calendar|calendar]], important in their [[Muisca agriculture|agriculture]].

Various astronomical sites have been constructed on the [[Altiplano Cundiboyacense]], the territories of the Muisca in the central [[Colombia]]n [[Andes]], but few remain today. Many archaeoastronomical places have been destroyed by the Spanish conquistadores and replaced by their [[catholicism|catholic]] churches. ''[[El Infiernito]]'', outside [[Villa de Leyva]], is the best known of the remaining sites. The [[Sun Temple (Sogamoso)|Temple of the Sun]] in sacred City of the Sun [[Sogamoso|Sugamuxi]] has been reconstructed.

Important [[List of Muisca scholars|scholars]] who have contributed to the knowledge of the Muisca astronomy were [[José Domingo Duquesne]] and [[Alexander von Humboldt]] in the late 18th and early 19th century and modern researchers as [[Eliécer Silva Celis]], [[Manuel Arturo Izquierdo Peña]], [[Carl Henrik Langebaek]] and Juan David Morales.

