{{Culture of Myanmar}}
'''Art of Myanmar''' refers to visual art created in [[Myanmar]] (Burma). Ancient Burmese art was influenced by India and China, and was often religious in nature, ranging from [[Hindu]] sculptures in the [[Thaton Kingdom]] to [[Theravada Buddhist]] images in the [[Sri Ksetra Kingdom]].<ref name=cooler2/> The Bagan period saw significant developments in many art forms, from wall paintings and sculptures to [[stucco]] and wood carving.<ref name=cooler2/> After a dearth of surviving art between the 14th and 16th centuries,<ref name=cooler3/> artists created paintings and sculptures that reflect Burmese culture.{{sfn|Raymond|2009|pp=144-157}} Burmese artists have been subjected to government interference and censorship, hindering the development of art in Myanmar.{{sfn|Ching|2011|pp=431-446}} Burmese art reflects the central Buddhist elements including the [[mudra]], [[Jataka tales]], the [[pagoda]], and [[Bodhisattva]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.burmese-buddhas.com/burma/buddhism-in-burma/|title=Introduction and history of Buddhism & Burmese Art in Burma|website=www.burmese-buddhas.com|language=en|access-date=2017-08-03|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180407053418/http://www.burmese-buddhas.com/burma/buddhism-in-burma/|archive-date=7 April 2018}}</ref> Throughout Burmese history, religious art or the royal court were the source of virtually all of Myanmar's visual arts.{{sfn|Galloway|2020|pp=136-147}}

{{Short description|Type of notational prefix in chemical nomenclature}}
In [[chemical nomenclature]], a '''descriptor''' is a notational prefix placed before the [[Systematic name|systematic substance name]], which describes the [[molecular configuration|configuration]] or the [[stereochemistry]] of the [[molecule]].<ref name="GoldBookRef stereodescriptor">{{GoldBookRef|title=stereodescriptor|file=S05976}}</ref> Some of the listed descriptors should not be used in [[publication]]s, as they no longer accurately correspond with the [[IUPAC nomenclature of chemistry|recommendations]] of the [[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry|IUPAC]]. Stereodescriptors are often used in combination with [[locant]]s to clearly identify a [[chemical structure]] unambiguously.

The descriptors, usually placed at the beginning of the systematic name, are not taken into account in the alphabetical sorting.

