'''Varanasi''' is a city that the Hindus all over the world consider as a holy city and a place of pilgrimage.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Havell|first1=E. B.|title=Benares the Sacred City|url=https://archive.org/details/benaressacredcit00have|date=1905|publisher=Blackie and Son|location=London|pages=[https://archive.org/details/benaressacredcit00have/page/226 226]|edition=1}}</ref> The centrality of this city in the [[Hindu]] worldview has a direct bearing upon its presence in various forms of literature, either directly upon the city itself, or having the city in a kind of central role. Many literary works mention [[Varanasi]] occasionally, but that will not be called city literature. The literary presence of the city of Varanasi or ''Kashi'', as it is invariably called in [[Sanskrit]] literature, can be conveniently understood by looking at the city literature divided into three groups, based on its language: [[Sanskrit]], [[Hindi]] and [[English language|English]].
